    {
      "site_id": "TMEM123-terminal_c-208-lit",
      "gene_symbol": "TMEM123",
      "uniprot_acc": "Q8N131",
      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "det_path": null,
      "site_kind": "terminal_c",
      "insert_after_residue": 208,
      "residue_before": "I",
      "residue_after": "",
      "residue_label": "I208",
      "topology_state": "I",
      "extracellular": false,
      "compartment": "intracellular",
      "tag_type": "other",
      "tag_length_aa": null,
      "linker": null,
      "evidence_type": "published tolerance of a different insertion (transposon, FP fusion)",
      "functional_impact_measured": "no",
      "confidence": "low",
      "rationale": "TMEM123 has a short intracellular C-terminus (residues 193-208, topology I). The snorkel tagging system (PMID 24023844) was specifically designed for membrane proteins with cytoplasmic C-termini: a heterologous TM domain followed by an extracellular epitope tag is appended to the cytoplasmic C-terminus, threading the tag to the extracellular face without inserting into the native ectodomain. TMEM123's topology (intracellular C-terminus after a single TM helix) is directly compatible with the snorkel design. This approach avoids any disruption to the mucin-like ectodomain critical for TMEM123's antiviral and immune functions. The snorkel system was validated for CD20 and ion channels with surface display confirmed by non-permeabilized antibody staining. [validation: not_measured; position: inferred; source: paper; entailment_verified: False]",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 24023844,
          "citation": "PMID 24023844"
        }
      ],
      "plddt": null,
      "conservation_rank": null,
      "median_conservation": null
    },
    {
      "site_id": "TMEM123-terminal_n-18-lit",
      "gene_symbol": "TMEM123",
      "uniprot_acc": "Q8N131",
      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "det_path": null,
      "site_kind": "terminal_n",
      "insert_after_residue": 18,
      "residue_before": "Q",
      "residue_after": "V",
      "residue_label": "Q18",
      "topology_state": "O",
      "extracellular": true,
      "compartment": "extracellular",
      "tag_type": "HA",
      "tag_length_aa": null,
      "linker": null,
      "evidence_type": "published tag insertion at this exact site",
      "functional_impact_measured": "yes",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "rationale": "Signal peptide spans residues 1-18 (18 consecutive S residues in computed topology); the mature extracellular N-terminus begins at V19. Inserting a short epitope tag after residue 18 (between Q18 and V19) places it immediately at the mature N-terminus on the extracellular face, more than 150 residues from the TM domain (residues 171-192). The SHREK study (PMID 34064525) purchased and used pTMEM123-HA from Sinobiological and demonstrated that TMEM123 retains antiviral activity (HIV-1 infectivity reduction), confirming the HA-tagged protein is functional. For a type I single-pass protein with an extracellular N-terminus, an N-terminal HA tag placed after SP cleavage is the standard Sinobiological configuration for surface-accessible tagging. This site avoids all TM residues, the cytoplasmic tail, and is far from the TM boundary. [validation: function_only; position: inferred; source: paper; entailment_verified: True]",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 34064525,
          "citation": "PMID 34064525"
        }
      ],
